       "round": 1,
       "summary": "The gathered materials provide a robust foundation for designing a 4-unit introductory course on game theory. Syllabi from multiple institutions (e.g., [L3oRewRFT], [aLZBsuFk5], [ZZGgu1UUW], [0bFZZPnJ8], [hBntm0Jml]) consistently emphasize a core structure covering normal-form games (strategies, payoffs, Nash equilibrium, mixed strategies) and extensive-form games (game trees, backward induction, subgame-perfect equilibrium). Advanced topics frequently included are repeated games (folk theorems), Bayesian games (incomplete information), and cooperative games (Shapley value, core). Popular textbooks identified across these courses include 'Games of Strategy' by Dixit, Skeath, and McAdams [HLwt6GyuP, q55DJ3P0N], 'Game Theory for Applied Economists' by Gibbons [L3oRewRFT, 0bFZZPnJ8], and 'Essentials of Game Theory' by Leyton-Brown and Shoham [aLZBsuFk5, ZZGgu1UUW]. Pedagogical approaches often combine formal mathematical modeling with interactive elements, such as online lab exercises (MobLab [L3oRewRFT]), in-class games [ZZGgu1UUW, q55DJ3P0N], and problem sets that focus on applying concepts to real-world scenarios like auctions, oligopoly models (Cournot/Bertrand), and bargaining. Mathematical prerequisites generally range from college-level algebra to mathematical maturity involving proofs, calculus, and probability [aLZBsuFk5, ZZGgu1UUW, hBntm0Jml]."
